This confirms it clearly. Commit `b83ce94c6` was a refactor replacing `DefaultSoundChannel.playSoundOnLocalMachine` calls with `ClipPlayer.play`, and in doing so it merged the previously-correct `if (...) {...} else if (...) {...}` structure for the move-sound logic into a single combined-condition `if`, dropping the separate combat-sound branch (which had played `CLIP_PHASE_MOVE_COMBAT` and set `m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove = true`). After this change, combat moves always played the non-combat sound and never set `m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove`, causing the sound to repeat on every combat move step instead of playing once — exactly the bug that commit `9a02e14e1` fixes (by re-splitting the condition and restoring the `m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove = true` assignment).

## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: b83ce94c63ec1891f5b76837455c8b2a53ad9d9c

## Reasoning
(1) I traced the history of the `m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove` logic in `TripleAPlayer.java` using `git log -p -S"m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove"`, which shows every commit that added/removed that token. This revealed the exact point where the logic structure changed from a correct `if/else if` into the flawed combined condition that the bug-fix commit (`9a02e14e1`) later reverts.

(2) In `b83ce94c6`, while refactoring sound-playing calls from `DefaultSoundChannel.playSoundOnLocalMachine` to `ClipPlayer.play`, the author also collapsed:
```java
if (nonCombat && !m_soundPlayedAlreadyNonCombatMove) {
  ...CLIP_PHASE_MOVE_NONCOMBAT...
  m_soundPlayedAlreadyNonCombatMove = true;
} else if (!nonCombat && !m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove) {
  ...CLIP_PHASE_MOVE_COMBAT...
  m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove = true;
}
```
into:
```java
if ((nonCombat && !m_soundPlayedAlreadyNonCombatMove) || (!nonCombat && !m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove)) {
  ClipPlayer.play(SoundPath.CLIP_PHASE_MOVE_NONCOMBAT, id.getName());
  m_soundPlayedAlreadyNonCombatMove = true;
}
```
This merge eliminated the combat-specific branch entirely: when a combat move occurred, the condition was still satisfied (via the `!nonCombat && !m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove` clause), but the body only ever set `m_soundPlayedAlreadyNonCombatMove = true` and never `m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove = true`. As a result, the combat-move sound guard flag was permanently stuck at `false`, causing the (wrong) non-combat sound to replay on every combat move step rather than playing once as intended.

(3) The bug-fixing commit `9a02e14e1` directly restores the split condition (`if (nonCombat...) {...} if (!nonCombat...) { ...; m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove = true; }`), which is structurally the inverse of what `b83ce94c6` did. This is strong, direct evidence that `b83ce94c6` is the bug-introducing commit — it's the only commit in the file's history where the once-per-move-type flag-setting logic was broken this way.

One caveat: the fix still plays `CLIP_PHASE_MOVE_NONCOMBAT` in both branches (rather than restoring `CLIP_PHASE_MOVE_COMBAT` for the combat case), so the "wrong sound" aspect of the bug is not fully fixed — only the "flag never set / sound repeats" aspect is. This doesn't change the identification of the introducing commit, since the fix commit's scope (as given) is about the flag/condition logic, which traces cleanly back to `b83ce94c6`.

